[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Test-Driven Development (TDD) in PHP"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Test-Driven Development (TDD) is a software development process that relies on the repetition of a very short development cycle: First the developer writes an (initially failing) automated test case that defines a desired improvement or new function, then produces the minimum amount of code to pass that test, and finally refactors the new code to acceptable standards. Kent Beck, who is credited with having developed or rediscovered the technique, introduced it to eXtreme Programming."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The TDD Cycle"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Write a test: Before writing any code, write a test that defines what the code should do."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Run the test: Run the test and see it fail. This confirms that the test is actually testing something."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Write the code: Write the minimum amount of code necessary to pass the test."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Run the test again: Run the test again and see it pass. If it doesn't pass, refactor the code until it does."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Refactor: Refactor the code to improve its design, while ensuring that the test still passes."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Setting up PHPUnit"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"PHPUnit is a popular testing framework for PHP. You can install it using Composer:"},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"composer require --dev phpunit/phpunit"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"After installing PHPUnit, you can create a 'phpunit.xml' configuration file in your project root to configure your test suite."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<phpunit bootstrap="vendor/autoload.php"
         colors="true">
    <testsuites>
        <testsuite name="My Project Test Suite">
            <directory>./tests/</directory>
        </testsuite>
    </testsuites>
    <php>
        <ini name="error_reporting" value="-1"/>
    </php>
</phpunit>"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Implementing a Simple Calculator"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Let's create a simple calculator class and use TDD to develop it."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Write the Test"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Create a 'tests' directory and a file named 'CalculatorTest.php' inside it."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"<?php

use PHPUnit\Framework\TestCase;

class CalculatorTest extends TestCase
{
    public function testAdd()
    {
        $calculator = new Calculator();
        $result = $calculator->add(2, 3);
        $this->assertEquals(5, $result);
    }
}"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Run the Test (and Watch it Fail)"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Run the test using the following command:"},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"./vendor/bin/phpunit tests/CalculatorTest.php"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"The test will fail because the 'Calculator' class and the 'add' method do not exist yet."},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Write the Code (to Pass the Test)"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"Create a 'Calculator.php' file in your project root with the following code:"},{"order":25,"type":"code","text":"<?php

class Calculator
{
    public function add(int $a, int $b): int
    {
        return $a + $b;
    }
}"},{"order":26,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Run the Test Again (and Watch it Pass)"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"Run the test again. This time, the test should pass."},{"order":28,"type":"subheading","text":"5. Refactor"},{"order":29,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this simple example, there's not much to refactor. However, in more complex scenarios, you would refactor the code to improve its design and maintainability, while ensuring that the tests still pass."},{"order":30,"type":"subheading","text":"Adding More Tests"},{"order":31,"type":"paragraph","text":"Let's add a test for the 'subtract' method."},{"order":32,"type":"code","text":"<?php

use PHPUnit\Framework\TestCase;

class CalculatorTest extends TestCase
{
    public function testAdd()
    {
        $calculator = new Calculator();
        $result = $calculator->add(2, 3);
        $this->assertEquals(5, $result);
    }

    public function testSubtract()
    {
        $calculator = new Calculator();
        $result = $calculator->subtract(5, 2);
        $this->assertEquals(3, $result);
    }
}"},{"order":33,"type":"paragraph","text":"Run the tests. The new test will fail. Implement the 'subtract' method in the 'Calculator' class:"},{"order":34,"type":"code","text":"<?php

class Calculator
{
    public function add(int $a, int $b): int
    {
        return $a + $b;
    }

    public function subtract(int $a, int $b): int
    {
        return $a - $b;
    }
}"},{"order":35,"type":"paragraph","text":"Run the tests again. Both tests should now pass."},{"order":36,"type":"subheading","text":"Benefits of TDD"},{"order":37,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Improved code quality: TDD forces you to think about the design of your code before you write it, leading to cleaner and more maintainable code."},{"order":38,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reduced debugging time: Tests act as documentation and help you catch bugs early in the development process."},{"order":39,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Increased confidence: Having a comprehensive suite of tests gives you confidence to make changes to your code without fear of breaking existing functionality."},{"order":40,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":41,"type":"paragraph","text":"Test-Driven Development is a valuable practice that can significantly improve the quality and maintainability of your PHP code. By writing tests first, you can ensure that your code meets the desired requirements and is less prone to errors."}]
